<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>Is Power BI cheaper than Tableau for a small business?</h3>
<p>In most small business setups, yes. Power BI Pro lists at $14 per user per month, and Power BI Desktop is free for building reports. Tableau lists Creator at $75, Explorer at $42 and Viewer at $15 per user per month, and every deployment needs at least one Creator seat. For a team of 10, Power BI usually costs less than half as much.</p>

<h3>Does Power BI work on a Mac?</h3>
<p>Power BI Desktop, the free authoring tool, runs on Windows only. Mac users can build and edit reports in the browser version, use a Windows virtual machine, or choose Tableau, whose desktop app runs on both Windows and Mac.</p>

<h3>Which is easier to learn, Power BI or Tableau?</h3>
<p>Power BI is usually easier for people who already use Excel, because Power Query and the formula language feel familiar. Tableau is often considered faster for exploring data visually by dragging and dropping, but it takes longer to master advanced calculations.</p>

<h3>Can a small business use both Power BI and Tableau?</h3>
<p>It is possible, but it is rarely worth it. Two tools mean two sets of licenses, two skill sets and two versions of your numbers. Pick one, build your core dashboards well, and revisit the decision only if a clear limit appears.</p>

